      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T17:32:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A hit on a cached `(slot-of o\u0027 i)` loads the validity cell and, if it\u0027s set, reads slot `i` of `o\u0027`. The cell is cleared by the library, and a library is untrusted code: any front end\u0027s runtime, an ES runtime included. If it fails to clear a cell, through a bug or on purpose, and `o\u0027` has since moved to dictionary mode (a delete of any property but the last-added one, item 3), the hit still reads slot `i`. Past the inline slots that\u0027s index `i - inline` of what `props` now points at: the dictionary table, not a slot vector. Its layout and length are different, so the read is out of bounds or reads table internals.\n\nThat makes memory safety depend on the library, which item 13 (\"no artifact has to be trusted\") and the consequence on line 147 (\"a bad hook is slow, not unsafe\") say it doesn\u0027t. The re-check on line 147 happens only when the entry is filled.\n\nA fix that keeps the hit cheap is to record `o\u0027`\u0027s shape in the entry and compare it on every hit. That\u0027s one more load and compare, and V8 checks the holder\u0027s map for the same reason. Then a stale cell can only give a stale but in-bounds value, which is the library\u0027s semantic bug and not a VM safety problem. Bounds-checking `i` against `o\u0027`\u0027s current shape on each hit would work too, but costs more than the compare.",
      "revId": "bf956b5c4c46618eb022b8040cfb6a10eb0bde8d",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
