      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T17:32:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Two lifetime points for the site tables, which live with a converted function outside the heap but hold heap references (effective methods, validity cells, and the `o\u0027` of a resolution):\n\n- **Cycle collection.** Trial deletion only sees edges it can describe. For a transient module (`eval`, `load_transient`), a cache entry that points at a closure of one of the module\u0027s own functions makes a cycle through a table the collector can\u0027t see, and the module is never freed. That\u0027s the same problem `load_transient` already refuses `call-direct` modules for (\"it would refer to its own module for ever\"). The table\u0027s references should be described as children of the module\u0027s heap object, or caching should be off in transient modules. \"Counted as any other heap reference\" (line 143) needs that to be true.\n- **Dependents lists.** Shapes, generics, classes and cells list the sites that cache them (item 12). When a function\u0027s table is freed (lazy conversion #55 makes that routine, as do transient modules), its sites have to leave those lists, or the lists need to hold sites weakly. Otherwise an invalidation writes into a freed table.",
      "revId": "bf956b5c4c46618eb022b8040cfb6a10eb0bde8d",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
